  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 430 AM EST FRI NOV 6 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Saturday evening with an upper-level low\nlocated over southern Ontario and the Lower Great Lakes. Broad\ncyclonic flow around the trough will direct a colder, drier airmass\ninto Kentucky from the northwest as surface high pressure builds in\nfrom the west. Calm to light winds under mostly clear skies would\ntypically favor frost formation if temperatures drop sufficiently.\n\nHowever, deterministic and ensemble guidance (including the RRFS,\nREFS, GFS, and ECMWF) remain noticeably milder than the NBM. The NBM\nappears to be weighting recent two-week observations where dry air\nand strong radiational decoupling produced colder valley lows. Given\npersistent CAA and lingering boundary layer mixing under\nnorthwesterly flow Saturday night, large ridge/valley temperature\nsplits are not favored. While NBM lows in the upper 20s to low 30s\nwere retained - which would yield widespread frost - confidence\nremains too low to warrant a Freeze Watch at this time. Should\nwarmer guidance in the mid-30s verify, patchy river valley fog may\ndevelop instead.\n\nHigh pressure settles overhead Sunday, bringing mostly sunny skies\nwith seasonable highs in the upper 50s to lower 60s. For Sunday\nnight, the forecast maintains a colder, decoupled solution with lows\ndropping into the lower 30s for much of the area. With high pressure\noverhead, clear skies and light winds will allow for efficient\nradiational cooling, which would support these colder valley\ntemperatures and result in areas of frost. However, forecast\nconfidence remains low: higher-resolution guidance, including the\nRRFS, suggests a more modified airmass with lows holding several\ndegrees warmer in the mid to upper 30s. Given this spread and the\nrecent NBM cold bias, the inherited colder numbers were kept for\ncontinuity. Should the warmer guidance verify, frost and possibly\nfreeze conditions could be limited to isolated cold spots, with \npatchy river valley fog becoming the prevailing overnight feature \ninstead.\n\nQuiet conditions will continue through Wednesday. Thereafter,\nguidance suggests another upper-level cyclone digging out of the\nUpper Midwest into the Great Lakes, with an attendant frontal\npassage across the Ohio and Mississippi Valleys bringing rain\nchances back to the area Thursday into Friday.",
